/* =====================================================================
   verb-gates-core.js — the model for ACTIVITY #123 of the one-by-one
   rebuild: "Vera's Verb Match" → THE VERB GATES (L.1.1.c; all 11
   locales).
   Design: docs/activities-rebuild/designs/123-vera-verb-match.md
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Every verb is a gate in the hedge on the path through the sentence.
   The subject's crowd (library pictures; the star means I) walks out of
   its house and meets the gate hung in the gap:
   · the TURNSTILE (is, was, has, runs…) lets exactly one through and
     locks — a crowd is left sitting behind it;
   · the DOUBLE GATE (are, were, have, run…) only opens when two or more
     push together — one alone waits in front of it;
   · the STAR DOOR (am; in the past only where the I-form differs: fr
     étais, it stavo, fi olin) spins open only for the star, alone. Where
     a level hangs no star door, the star walks through the turnstile.
   · sv / da / no: the verb does not agree, so the gates are the predicate
     adjective — turnstile = common singular (stor), the t-turnstile =
     neuter singular (stort; it turns only for an ett/et-word), double
     gate = plural (stora / store).
   Only the right gate lets the whole crowd through. Five levels of nine
   rounds (sv/da/no three); the plan reshuffles the order per pass, never
   three rounds in a row with the same right gate, never the same subject
   twice in a row.
   DATA is written by a build script. Pure and DOM-free; UMD
   (window.VerbGatesCore / require()).
   ===================================================================== */
